  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-05-20T00:51:47.256Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "There is no purpose for a publicly-visible username except to worsen security for users by exposing their login name. It can create unintended, accidental, and immutable connections to one's identity, and VRChat provides no recourse except to destroy and rebuild one's online presence should a violation of privacy require it.\n\nI think the best solution to this would be to hide the login from the website and entirely from the API (as malicious client users could easily pick out login name <-> user id/display name mappings otherwise).\n\nConcretely:\n\n1 VRChat support isn't able to process username change requests. (verbatim from VRChat support)\n\n1a Assuming this to be true as stated, this means that even in the case of personally identifying information (PII), the username won't change.\n1b Assuming this was incorrectly stated or the exception was omitted for simplicity, the standard of what correctly constitutes PII is not fixed, subject to disagreement, and presumed to be intractable to create a detailed standard for. Presumably, allowing appeals to be processed would complexify the process and increase the workload for support staff.\n\n2 There is no (or insufficient) indication, prior to registration, that the visibility of the login username is both public and permanent.\n3 There is no need for the username as a public and stable textual identifier, as the user ids (usr_*) are a valid and stable unique identifier for users, nor any positive use case, but very obvious downsides (i.e. account compromise).",
